gh_api '{
  "title": "[Migration] Dubins Path Computation (DubinsPath, DubinsPathSelector, DubinsPathConstraints)",
  "labels": ["migration","core"],
  "body": "## Code Review Summary\n`DubinsPath` and its six concrete subclasses (`LslDubinsPath`, `RsrDubinsPath`, `RslDubinsPath`, `LsrDubinsPath`, `RlrDubinsPath`, `LrlDubinsPath`) implement the classical Dubins shortest-path algorithm for a vehicle with a minimum turning radius. `DubinsPathConstraints` captures start/goal `GeoCoordDir` and the radius constraint. The abstract base lazily computes three arc/line segment lengths (`length1`, `length2`, `length3`, `total_length`) on first access using a protected `ComputeTangents` override. `OuterDubinsPath` (LSL, RSR) uses outer tangents; `InnerDubinsPath` (RSL, LSR) uses inner tangents with angle computed from the law of cosines; `CurvedDubinsPath` (RLR, LRL) uses a third intermediate circle. `DubinsPathSelector` computes all six candidate paths and returns the shortest valid one.\n\n## Source Files\n- `SourceCode/AgOpenGPS.Core/Models/Guidance/DubinsPath.cs`\n- `SourceCode/AgOpenGPS.Core/Models/Guidance/DubinsPathConstraints.cs`\n- `SourceCode/AgOpenGPS.Core/Models/Guidance/DubinsPathSelector.cs`\n\n## What Must Be Converted\nThe abstract `DubinsPath` base with lazy computation via `_is_computed` flag; the `_compute_tangents` abstract method; the three concrete base classes (`OuterDubinsPath`, `InnerDubinsPath`, `CurvedDubinsPath`) each providing the tangent computation; and the six leaf classes as simple subclasses that specify their `TurnType` triplets. `DubinsPathSelector.select(constraints)` must instantiate all six paths, filter those where `path_is_possible(start_circle, goal_circle)` is `True`, and return the one with minimum `total_length`.\n\n## Python Implementation Requirements\n- Use `abc.ABC` and `@abstractmethod` for `_compute_tangents`.\n- Lazy computation via `functools.cached_property` or explicit `_computed` flag (match C# lazy pattern).\n- `TurnType` enum reused from issue #1.\n- `DubinsPathConstraints` as a frozen `dataclass` holding `start: GeoCoordDir`, `goal: GeoCoordDir`, `radius: float`.\n- `DubinsPathSelector.select` returns `DubinsPath | None`.\n- Pure Python `math` module — no `numpy` dependency.\n- Full type hints.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All six concrete path types compute correct `total_length` for a canonical test scenario.\n- [ ] `DubinsPathSelector.select` returns the shortest of the feasible paths.\n- [ ] `OuterDubinsPath.path_is_possible` returns `False` when circles are identical.\n- [ ] `InnerDubinsPath.path_is_possible` returns `False` when circles overlap.\n- [ ] `CurvedDubinsPath.path_is_possible` returns `False` when circles are too far apart.\n- [ ] Lazy computation: `total_length` only computed once even when accessed multiple times.\n- [ ] All public functions have type hints.\n- [ ] pytest tests with known geometric inputs.\n- [ ] Code passes ruff linting.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Issue #1 (Core Geospatial Primitive Types — `GeoCoord`, `GeoDir`, `GeoCoordDir`, `GeoCircle`, `GeoDelta`, `TurnType`).\n\n## Notes\n- The six Dubins path types form the turn-generation backbone of `CYouTurn` (GPS project) — correctness is safety-critical.\n- The C# code uses `Debug.Assert` to validate `TurnType` — replace with `ValueError` in Python in non-test contexts."
}'
