gh_api '{
  "title": "[Migration] Math Geometry Utilities (glm static class, vec2, vec3)",
  "labels": ["migration","core"],
  "body": "## Code Review Summary\nThe GPS project defines `vec2` (easting, northing) and `vec3` (easting, northing, heading) as simple C# classes used throughout the legacy GPS codebase alongside the newer `GeoCoord`/`GeoDir` types from the Core project. The `glm` static class provides geometry utilities: `IsZero`, `InRangeBetweenAB` (point on line segment), `IsPointInPolygon` (ray-casting, four overloads for `List<vec3>`/`List<vec2>` combinations). `CGLM` in AgIO is a duplicate of this utility class. These types predate the `AgOpenGPS.Core` geospatial types and are still used in the GPS application classes (`CABLine`, `CYouTurn`, `CBoundary`, etc.).\n\n## Source Files\n- `SourceCode/GPS/Classes/vec3.cs`\n- `SourceCode/GPS/Classes/CGLM.cs`\n- `SourceCode/AgIO/Source/Classes/CGLM.cs`\n\n## What Must Be Converted\n`vec2` and `vec3` lightweight point types and all `glm` utility functions. The Python equivalents should bridge toward the Core `GeoCoord`/`GeoDir` types — consider providing conversion helpers. Avoid `numpy` for this low-level utility module.\n\n## Python Implementation Requirements\n- `Vec2` and `Vec3` as frozen dataclasses with `easting: float`, `northing: float`, and optionally `heading: float`.\n- `is_zero(value: float, tolerance: float = 1e-6) -> bool`.\n- `in_range_between_ab(start, end, point) -> bool` (point-on-segment test).\n- `is_point_in_polygon(polygon: list[Vec3 | Vec2], test_point: Vec3 | Vec2) -> bool` using ray-casting.\n- Conversion helpers `vec2_to_geo_coord(v: Vec2) -> GeoCoord` and `vec3_to_geo_coord_dir(v: Vec3) -> GeoCoordDir`.\n- Module lives at `agopengps/gps/math_utils.py`.\n- Full type hints.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `is_point_in_polygon` matches C# `glm.IsPointInPolygon` for both convex and concave polygons.\n- [ ] `in_range_between_ab` returns `True` for midpoints and `False` for points outside the segment.\n- [ ] `vec2_to_geo_coord` and `vec3_to_geo_coord_dir` produce numerically identical values.\n- [ ] All public functions have type hints.\n- [ ] pytest tests for all overloads using known geometric inputs.\n- [ ] Code passes ruff linting.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Issue #1 (Geospatial Primitives — for conversion helpers).\n\n## Notes\n- `vec2` and `vec3` are legacy types that should be phased out in favour of `GeoCoord`/`GeoCoordDir` over time — provide conversion helpers to ease this migration.\n- The `glm` class name conflicts with the popular C++ GLM library — rename to `geo_utils` or `math_utils` in Python.\n- `CGLM` in AgIO is identical to `glm` in GPS — merge into a single module."
}'
