gh_api '{
  "title": "[Migration] GPS Simulator (CSim) and Module Simulator (ModSim/FormSim)",
  "labels": ["migration","core"],
  "body": "## Code Review Summary\n`CSim` in the GPS project provides software GPS simulation: it advances a virtual vehicle position along a heading, generates synthetic NMEA sentences (GGA, VTG), and feeds them into the NMEA parser as if they came from real hardware. It supports configurable speed, simulated fix quality, and satellite count. The position advances using `sin/cos` of the heading angle per timer tick. `ModSim/FormSim` is a separate Windows Forms application that simulates the steer/machine hardware modules over UDP, generating wheel-angle-sensor (WAS) feedback and responding to section-control PGN packets — used for software testing without physical hardware.\n\n## Source Files\n- `SourceCode/GPS/Classes/CSim.cs`\n- `SourceCode/ModSim/Source/Forms/FormSim.cs`\n\n## What Must Be Converted\n`CSim` as a Python class that generates synthetic NMEA sentences on a timer. `FormSim` as a headless async service that listens for PGN packets and responds with simulated sensor data.\n\n## Python Implementation Requirements\n- `GpsSimulator` class in `agopengps/gps/simulator.py`: `start(config: SimConfig)`, `stop()`, `on_nmea_sentence: Callable[[str], None]` callback.\n- `SimConfig` frozen dataclass: `start_lat`, `start_lon`, `start_heading_deg`, `speed_kmh`, `fix_quality`, `satellites`.\n- Position advances at `asyncio` timer ticks using haversine bearing-distance projection.\n- `ModuleSimulator` async service in `agopengps/modsim/simulator.py`: responds to section-control PGN packets with simulated WAS feedback bytes.\n- No Windows Forms.\n- Full type hints.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `GpsSimulator` generates valid GGA sentences with advancing position at configured speed.\n- [ ] Position advances in the correct direction for the configured heading.\n- [ ] `ModuleSimulator` responds to steer PGN with WAS feedback bytes.\n- [ ] `stop()` cancels all tasks cleanly.\n- [ ] All public functions have type hints.\n- [ ] pytest tests using `asyncio` test patterns.\n- [ ] Code passes ruff linting.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Issue #9 (NMEA Parser), Issue #19 (PGN Protocol).\n\n## Notes\n- `CSim` uses `Math.Sin(heading) * speed * dt` for position update — ensure the coordinate convention (easting = sin, northing = cos of heading measured from north) matches the Core `GeoDir` convention.\n- `ModSim` is used for hardware-in-the-loop testing — preserve it as a testable standalone service."
}'
